      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When a signal is truly chaotic, like the noise generated by a poorly maintained machine, the Fourier Transform still works, but the resulting spectrum will look very different. Instead of sharp, distinct peaks, you'll see a broad, continuous distribution of energy across many frequencies. This continuous \"hiss\" in the spectrum *is* the representation of the chaos—it means the energy isn't concentrated in a few predictable tones, but is smeared out across the entire frequency range.\n\nEven in chaotic signals, the transform doesn't fail; it just tells you that the energy is distributed broadly, which is still valuable information. It tells you *how* chaotic it is by showing the shape and spread of that energy distribution. If you had a signal that was mostly predictable but had a few sudden, sharp spikes (like a single, loud *thunk*), the transform would show those spikes as very high, narrow peaks across a wide range of frequencies, indicating transient, non-periodic events.",
